WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.147
Lesson one point three.

2
00:00:01.435 --> 00:00:03.071
The loop that runs the drums live.

3
00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.161
The guitarist plays in real time, whenever

4
00:00:09.161 --> 00:00:09.639
they like.

5
00:00:10.079 --> 00:00:12.556
The laptop keeps its own clock: a grid of

6
00:00:12.556 --> 00:00:12.872
slots.

7
00:00:13.189 --> 00:00:15.403
The tall marks are downbeats, the first

8
00:00:15.403 --> 00:00:16.562
sixteenth of each bar.

9
00:00:17.065 --> 00:00:19.829
Slot t sounds at S t: the first downbeat,

10
00:00:19.829 --> 00:00:21.634
S nought, plus t slot lengths.

11
00:00:21.973 --> 00:00:24.343
By default the laptop starts at a hundred

12
00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:26.486
beats per minute, so a slot lasts a

13
00:00:26.486 --> 00:00:28.235
hundred and fifty milliseconds.

14
00:00:28.574 --> 00:00:30.774
Its job is to fit the guitar onto this

15
00:00:30.774 --> 00:00:31.056
grid.

16
00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.487
First, when was the string actually

17
00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:36.890
plucked?

18
00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:39.362
The packet arrives a little later than the

19
00:00:39.362 --> 00:00:41.480
pluck, so the laptop can't just use the

20
00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:42.287
time it arrived.

21
00:00:42.732 --> 00:00:44.846
If the board has sent a clock packet,

22
00:00:44.846 --> 00:00:47.066
called SYNC, in the last second, that

23
00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:48.334
lines the two clocks up.

24
00:00:48.651 --> 00:00:50.871
Otherwise the laptop uses the board's own

25
00:00:50.871 --> 00:00:53.302
stamp on each onset: it lines the clocks

26
00:00:53.302 --> 00:00:55.416
up from the quickest packet in the last

27
00:00:55.416 --> 00:00:57.742
twenty seconds, then takes off an assumed

28
00:00:57.742 --> 00:00:59.063
twenty-millisecond delay.

29
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.706
If the board's clock looks wrong, it uses

30
00:01:01.706 --> 00:01:03.926
the arrival time, minus the same twenty

31
00:01:03.926 --> 00:01:04.613
milliseconds.

32
00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.202
Any estimate more than a second before the

33
00:01:07.202 --> 00:01:09.052
packet arrived, or more than two

34
00:01:09.052 --> 00:01:10.955
milliseconds after, is thrown out.

35
00:01:12.718 --> 00:01:15.185
Then the pluck is placed: how many slots

36
00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:17.492
after the first downbeat, rounded to the

37
00:01:17.492 --> 00:01:19.476
nearest whole slot, exactly as the

38
00:01:19.476 --> 00:01:20.442
training data was.

39
00:01:20.763 --> 00:01:23.177
A pluck one point three one seconds in, at

40
00:01:23.177 --> 00:01:25.591
a hundred beats per minute, is eight point

41
00:01:25.591 --> 00:01:26.717
seven three slots in.

42
00:01:27.039 --> 00:01:28.326
So it goes in slot nine.

43
00:01:29.105 --> 00:01:31.536
Each drum decision reads one guitar slot,

44
00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:32.310
and closes it.

45
00:01:32.642 --> 00:01:34.742
If a note turns up after its slot was

46
00:01:34.742 --> 00:01:37.284
read, it moves to the next open slot, and

47
00:01:37.284 --> 00:01:38.113
is marked late.

48
00:01:38.725 --> 00:01:40.625
Late notes are counted on the page.

49
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.176
And if the board later takes a note back

50
00:01:43.176 --> 00:01:45.728
as a false onset, with a VOID packet, it's

51
00:01:45.728 --> 00:01:48.062
removed, if its slot hasn't been decided

52
00:01:48.062 --> 00:01:48.279
yet.

53
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:53.863
To play along with the guitarist rather

54
00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:56.208
than a click, the laptop can listen for a

55
00:01:56.208 --> 00:01:57.914
count-in: four even strums.

56
00:01:58.234 --> 00:02:00.580
Here is one, recorded with the fake board.

57
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:03.052
A straight line is fitted through the four

58
00:02:03.052 --> 00:02:05.258
strums, the one that misses them least: a

59
00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:06.113
least-squares line.

60
00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:08.634
Its slope is the beat length, p: here just

61
00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:10.434
under five hundred milliseconds, so a

62
00:02:10.434 --> 00:02:12.055
hundred and twenty beats per minute.

63
00:02:12.325 --> 00:02:14.306
The line's next point, one beat after the

64
00:02:14.306 --> 00:02:16.376
fourth strum, becomes the first downbeat,

65
00:02:16.376 --> 00:02:17.637
and the drums come in there.

66
00:02:18.054 --> 00:02:19.988
A strum is any group of notes within

67
00:02:19.988 --> 00:02:22.131
eighty milliseconds, so a chord counts

68
00:02:22.131 --> 00:02:22.392
once.

69
00:02:22.706 --> 00:02:25.057
The gaps must be even, each within fifteen

70
00:02:25.057 --> 00:02:27.305
percent of the middle one, and the tempo

71
00:02:27.305 --> 00:02:29.395
must be between sixty and a hundred and

72
00:02:29.395 --> 00:02:29.761
eighty.

73
00:02:30.075 --> 00:02:31.590
If not, it keeps listening.

74
00:02:32.081 --> 00:02:33.731
This has run with the fake board.

75
00:02:34.031 --> 00:02:36.131
It hasn't yet been tried with a guitarist.

76
00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:41.787
The count-in fixes a tempo, but players

77
00:02:41.787 --> 00:02:42.107
drift.

78
00:02:42.426 --> 00:02:44.397
So the grid can follow the guitarist.

79
00:02:44.716 --> 00:02:46.953
Picture the grid as a comb of eighth-note

80
00:02:46.953 --> 00:02:47.273
lines.

81
00:02:47.593 --> 00:02:49.989
Around each line is a gate, twelve percent

82
00:02:49.989 --> 00:02:52.120
of a beat wide on each side: sixty

83
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.090
milliseconds at a hundred and twenty.

84
00:02:54.629 --> 00:02:56.690
A strum lands twenty-five milliseconds

85
00:02:56.690 --> 00:02:57.588
after beat eight.

86
00:02:57.905 --> 00:03:00.231
Its error, e, is how far it is from the

87
00:03:00.231 --> 00:03:02.134
nearest line: plus twenty-five

88
00:03:02.134 --> 00:03:04.353
milliseconds, five hundredths of a beat.

89
00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:06.732
That's inside the gate, so it's used.

90
00:03:07.178 --> 00:03:07.971
Two things change.

91
00:03:08.236 --> 00:03:10.263
The period, the time between beats, grows

92
00:03:10.263 --> 00:03:12.334
by a twentieth of the error: five hundred

93
00:03:12.334 --> 00:03:14.053
becomes five hundred and one point two

94
00:03:14.053 --> 00:03:15.992
five milliseconds, a hundred and nineteen

95
00:03:15.992 --> 00:03:17.270
point seven beats per minute.

96
00:03:17.534 --> 00:03:19.561
And the phase, where the lines sit, moves

97
00:03:19.561 --> 00:03:21.633
a fifth of the way toward the strum: five

98
00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:22.205
milliseconds.

99
00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.409
So one strum nudges the grid, rather than

100
00:03:24.409 --> 00:03:24.894
yanking it.

101
00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.548
A strum that lands far from any line, like

102
00:03:27.548 --> 00:03:29.655
a sixteenth-note fill, is outside every

103
00:03:29.655 --> 00:03:30.809
gate, and is ignored.

104
00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.167
Each change to the period is also capped

105
00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.174
at three percent, and the beat length

106
00:03:35.174 --> 00:03:36.930
stays within twenty percent of the

107
00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.138
count-in's: a hundred to a hundred and

108
00:03:39.138 --> 00:03:41.295
fifty beats per minute after a count-in at

109
00:03:41.295 --> 00:03:42.349
a hundred and twenty.

110
00:03:43.815 --> 00:03:45.468
The tracker is a kind of phase-locked

111
00:03:45.468 --> 00:03:45.685
loop.

112
00:03:45.946 --> 00:03:47.817
Here it is on a simulated player, steady

113
00:03:47.817 --> 00:03:49.644
at a hundred and sixteen beats per minute

114
00:03:49.644 --> 00:03:51.428
after a count-in at a hundred and twenty.

115
00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:53.647
At first each strum comes later and later,

116
00:03:53.647 --> 00:03:54.909
up to forty-six milliseconds.

117
00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.084
The grid's tempo eases down, overshoots a

118
00:03:57.084 --> 00:03:59.042
little, and by strum twenty is within half

119
00:03:59.042 --> 00:04:00.564
a beat per minute of a hundred and

120
00:04:00.564 --> 00:04:02.044
sixteen, still creeping back up.

121
00:04:02.417 --> 00:04:04.598
In a recorded fake-board take, twenty-six

122
00:04:04.598 --> 00:04:06.383
of sixty-one strums passed the gate.

123
00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.712
The constants were chosen by hand, not

124
00:04:08.712 --> 00:04:09.010
tuned.

125
00:04:09.307 --> 00:04:11.438
On replays of Sena's playing it put thirty

126
00:04:11.438 --> 00:04:13.570
to thirty-nine percent of his notes within

127
00:04:13.570 --> 00:04:15.750
twenty milliseconds of the lines, against

128
00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.783
twenty-seven to thirty-three holding one

129
00:04:17.783 --> 00:04:18.080
tempo.

130
00:04:18.377 --> 00:04:20.360
It hasn't yet run live with a guitarist.

131
00:04:23.773 --> 00:04:25.923
Now one slot, in the order the code does

132
00:04:25.923 --> 00:04:26.073
it.

133
00:04:26.373 --> 00:04:28.323
First the loop sleeps until the slot's

134
00:04:28.323 --> 00:04:30.573
decision time, which the next lesson works

135
00:04:30.573 --> 00:04:30.773
out.

136
00:04:31.294 --> 00:04:33.211
If a new drummer was asked for and this is

137
00:04:33.211 --> 00:04:34.147
a downbeat, switch.

138
00:04:34.515 --> 00:04:36.426
If that starts a new segment, wipe the

139
00:04:36.426 --> 00:04:37.126
model's memory.

140
00:04:37.545 --> 00:04:39.693
Read the guitar's slot t minus one, and

141
00:04:39.693 --> 00:04:40.358
mark it used.

142
00:04:40.756 --> 00:04:42.472
Build the five numbers from lesson one

143
00:04:42.472 --> 00:04:42.912
point two.

144
00:04:43.305 --> 00:04:45.325
Step the model once: nine probabilities.

145
00:04:45.694 --> 00:04:47.815
Compare each with its drum's threshold:

146
00:04:47.815 --> 00:04:48.711
nine hits or rests.

147
00:04:49.086 --> 00:04:50.512
Let the crash rule add a crash where it

148
00:04:50.512 --> 00:04:50.762
should.

149
00:04:51.091 --> 00:04:52.186
And step the bass, if it's on.

150
00:04:52.531 --> 00:04:54.717
Then the hits are scheduled to sound at S

151
00:04:54.717 --> 00:04:57.006
t, the slot is written to disk, and the

152
00:04:57.006 --> 00:04:57.683
pages update.

153
00:04:57.995 --> 00:04:59.348
All of that, every slot.

154
00:05:02.827 --> 00:05:04.054
Here are eighteen bars of a take.

155
00:05:04.428 --> 00:05:06.192
If you ask for a different drummer

156
00:05:06.192 --> 00:05:08.460
mid-bar, it waits for the next downbeat,

157
00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.828
and starts a new segment: fresh memory,

158
00:05:10.828 --> 00:05:13.045
and its first slot hears silence, just as

159
00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:14.356
every training song began.

160
00:05:14.807 --> 00:05:16.579
On top of the model sits a small crash

161
00:05:16.579 --> 00:05:16.807
rule.

162
00:05:17.079 --> 00:05:19.033
A drummer's first downbeat is a kick and a

163
00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:21.169
crash, and nothing else, because one model

164
00:05:21.169 --> 00:05:23.031
fired all nine drums on a fresh start,

165
00:05:23.031 --> 00:05:24.258
twelve times out of twelve.

166
00:05:24.531 --> 00:05:26.530
Every eight bars, a kick and crash with a

167
00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:28.211
coin flip: probability one half.

168
00:05:28.484 --> 00:05:30.301
And when the guitar comes back after at

169
00:05:30.301 --> 00:05:32.392
least a bar of silence, on the next beat,

170
00:05:32.392 --> 00:05:33.755
with probability seven tenths.

171
00:05:34.144 --> 00:05:35.947
The coin flips come from a random

172
00:05:35.947 --> 00:05:38.280
generator started from a recorded number,

173
00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:40.295
a seed, and they happen only at set

174
00:05:40.295 --> 00:05:42.787
moments: every eight bars, and when the

175
00:05:42.787 --> 00:05:43.742
guitar comes back.

176
00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.446
So the same playing, the same switches and

177
00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:48.196
the same seed give the same take.

178
00:05:51.657 --> 00:05:53.904
Every take writes a session folder: the

179
00:05:53.904 --> 00:05:56.001
settings, every packet as it arrived,

180
00:05:56.001 --> 00:05:58.348
every slot's numbers and hits, the events,

181
00:05:58.348 --> 00:05:59.047
and the sound.

182
00:05:59.536 --> 00:06:01.869
Afterwards, the same model is run again

183
00:06:01.869 --> 00:06:04.369
offline on the recorded numbers, and every

184
00:06:04.369 --> 00:06:06.591
probability and every hit must come out

185
00:06:06.591 --> 00:06:07.757
equal, bit for bit.

186
00:06:08.091 --> 00:06:09.313
Not close: equal.

187
00:06:09.929 --> 00:06:12.255
A tracked take: two thousand three hundred

188
00:06:12.255 --> 00:06:13.854
and four cells, none different.

189
00:06:14.145 --> 00:06:16.179
And offline, the genre drummers stepped

190
00:06:16.179 --> 00:06:18.263
one slot at a time against whole chunks,

191
00:06:18.263 --> 00:06:20.491
on two songs each: none of a hundred and

192
00:06:20.491 --> 00:06:22.042
eleven thousand cells different.

193
00:06:22.333 --> 00:06:24.513
One honest limit: the check re-runs the

194
00:06:24.513 --> 00:06:26.209
model, not the crash rule on top.

195
00:06:27.832 --> 00:06:30.244
So the loop places each pluck on the grid,

196
00:06:30.244 --> 00:06:32.817
decides each slot, plays it, and logs it,

197
00:06:32.817 --> 00:06:33.942
so it can be checked.

198
00:06:34.264 --> 00:06:36.676
Next: exactly how much time all of that

199
00:06:36.676 --> 00:06:36.890
has.

